 ],
 "literal": "Even if it angered, even if it raged, / Even if it attacked, there would be no escape indeed! / Like singers, joining hands in worship, praising, / It must have begged! The one with a golden garland, / Who has an army that conquers raging battles, / Valavan of the strong chariot, Death took him indeed!",
 "translation": "Even if Death had shown anger, even if it had raged, even if it had attacked, it would not have escaped alive! So, Death must have come like a bard, joining its hands in worship, singing praises, and begging for him. That is how Death took Valavan of the strong chariots, who has an army that conquers raging battles, and who wears a golden garland.",
 "modern": "கூற்றுவன் சினந்து வந்திருந்தாலும், கோபித்து வந்திருந்தாலும், தாக்கியிருந்தாலும் அவனுக்குத் தப்பிப்பிழைத்தல் என்பது இருந்திருக்காது. எனவே, பாணர்களைப் போலக் கைகளைக் குவித்துத் தொழுது, புகழ்ந்து பாடி, இரந்துதான் பெற்றிருக்க வேண்டும். பொன்னாலான மாலையையும், நெருங்கிப் போர் செய்யும் போர்க்களங்களை வெல்லும் படையையும், வலிமையான தேரையும் உடைய வளவனைத் தன்னுள் கொண்ட கூற்றுவன்!",
 "explanation": "This poem is a moving eulogy (kaiyaṟunilai) on the death of the Chola king Killi Valavan, who died at Kulumuṟṟam, composed by the poet Māṟōkkattu Nappacalaiyār. The poet expresses deep grief and admiration for the king's unmatched valor. She asserts that Death (Kūṟṟu) could never have defeated the king in a direct battle; had Death tried to attack or rage against him, Death itself would not have survived. Therefore, the poet reasons, Death must have approached the king disguised as a humble bard, praising him with folded hands and begging for his life as a gift. This clever imagery highlights the king's supreme power in battle and his legendary generosity, showing that he would give away even his own life if begged for it.",
